A||N||3||Gold-86SE||Brake Fade. Why? Help me... Please.||||1||
Z||000000||Gold-86SE||05-22-2001||12:10 AM||||<BR>Guys, just finished replacing all four corners with new calipers and new pads.  Calipers are the standard rebuilt replacements from AutoZone (rear) and O'Rielly (front).  I Bled the system and it appeared to releave all air from the system.<P>Here is the problem, if I let the brakes go untouched for a few moments then try to apply the brakes I have no pedal.  However, if I pump the pedal I have pressure.<P>Took it for a minor road test and blamo... nada... zip... zero. Had to use first gear to stop.<P>Prior to the brake job, I had perfect pedal feel, with no fade, just a noisy right front caliper.<P>What did I do? What happened? I am at a loss and really want to be back on the road.<P>------------------<BR>84 SE 4.10 4 Speed (Not So Fair Weather Driver)<BR>85 GT Automatic (Restoration Project)<BR>86 SE 4 Speed (Fair Weather Daily Driver)<BR>87 SC 5 Speed (Not Running, V8 Conversion?)<BR>Please visit my <A HREF="http://www.trifocus.net/~fws/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Work Shop</A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||BV MotorSports||05-22-2001||12:16 AM||||Go over your entire brake system inch by inch. Take a good look for leaks around the new calipers and the master cylinder. You may even have an internal leak from the m/c to booster. If you dont find anything take your car to a shop and have the system power flushed and filled. <BR>I just recently added braided brake lines and new calipers to my 87 TSi and thought I had a good pedal....took it to get the brake system pwr flushed and I have a rock hard brake pedal now! Difference is night and day.<P>Steven ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jake_Dragon||05-22-2001||06:32 AM||||Buy a hand vacuum pump and make sure you bleed in the proper order. You could also let it gravity bleed for while first, just jack up the front of the car a little and open the bleeders till you start getting fluid from your caliper. Then use the pump.<BR>I have excellent breaks and that is what I did.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://home1.gte.net/rebel33/logo.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||05-22-2001||09:17 AM||||For the record.... This is NOT a fade problem... It's a bleeding problem. Fade is a whole diferant problem usually caused by over heating.<P><BR>Did you take off the MC or otherwise let air into it? If so you'll have a hard time bleeding on the car. read my brake articles...<P><BR>The rear calipers can be dificult to bleed because of the way the hand brake works.<P>You can try taking the caliper off the bracket and CAREFULLY turn it so the piston face is pointing down. Tap the caliper woth a plastic hammer ow wood block to shake air out of the piston.<P>BEFORE YOU PUMP THE PEDAL! Put a block of wood in the caliper to keep it from spitting out the piston... <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-22-2001).]||||reg||1
